<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Faisal II of Iraq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1935">1935</a>–<a href="/years/1958">1958</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Faisal II was the last King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a> as a child in 1939, with his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-ilah">Abd al-Ilah</a> as regent until 1953. His governments, dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuri-al-said">Nuri al-Said</a>, were closely tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad-pact">Baghdad Pact</a>. In February 1958 he formed the Arab Federation with his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-of-jordan">Hussein of Jordan</a>, but on 14 July 1958 army officers led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-karim-qasim">Abd al-Karim Qasim</a> overthrew the monarchy and killed him and most of his family.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/faisal-ii-of-iraq.jpg" alt="Faisal II of Iraq" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iraqi Government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_of_Iraq_-_Faisal_II_1953.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Faisal was the only son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and Queen Aliya, a daughter of 'Ali bin Hussein, King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>. His father was killed in a car crash when Faisal was three, and he came to the throne on 4 April 1939, with Prince '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-ilah">Abd al-Ilah</a> as regent. In April 1941 a military coup aimed at aligning Iraq with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> briefly deposed the regent. It led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-iraqi-war">Anglo-Iraqi War</a>, and a combined Allied force restored 'Abd al-Ilah. During the Second World War Faisal lived for a time with his mother in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkshire">Berkshire</a> and attended Harrow School with Prince Hussein, the future King of Jordan, with whom he formed a close friendship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Faisal came of age on 2 May 1953, the same day Hussein was enthroned in Jordan. He relied at first on '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-ilah">Abd al-Ilah</a> and on the veteran politician Nuri al-Sa'id. The Anglo-Iraqi Treaty of 1948 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad-pact">Baghdad Pact</a> of 1955, which kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> involved in Iraqi affairs, met large protests that left hundreds of demonstrators dead. Oil revenues grew in the 1950s after negotiations with the Iraq Petroleum Company in 1952, and a Development Board received a share of them. It drew up plans for a Greater Baghdad, and architects including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-gropius">Walter Gropius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-corbusier">Le Corbusier</a> were invited to design buildings there.</p><p>Opposition widened. There were uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a> and Hayy in 1956, and in February 1957 a Front of National Union brought together the National Democrats, Independents, Communists and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baath-party">Ba'ath Party</a>, while a Supreme Committee of Free Officers formed in the army. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a> on 1 February 1958, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and Jordan declared the Arab Hashemite Union on 14 February, with Faisal as head of state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In July 1958 Iraqi army units under Colonel Abdul-Karim Qasim, sent towards Jordan during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> crisis, marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> instead and carried out a coup on 14 July. Faisal ordered the Royal Guard to offer no resistance. At the Rihab Palace the king, '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-ilah">Abd al-Ilah</a> and other members of the family were gathered and, according to the account given, fired on as they crossed the kitchen garden. Faisal was shot in the head and neck, and only Princess Hiyam survived. The 14 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> ended the thirty-seven-year-old Hashemite monarchy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> became a republic. Faisal was later reburied in a marble tomb next to his father in the Royal Cemetery in Baghdad.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Faisal II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In response, the Hashemite cousins King <b>Faisal II</b> of the Kingdom of Iraq and King Hussein of the Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan proclaimed the Arab Federation on 14 February 1958.</blockquote><a href="/state/arab-federation">Arab Federation</a></li><li><blockquote>Faisal I died in 1933 and was followed by Ghazi and the boy king <b>Faisal II</b>, under the regent Abd al-Ilah.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-iraq">Kingdom of Iraq</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in Al Rehab Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Faisal II of Iraq"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6016 4653h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6016 4653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
